th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Math-IV-Multivariate-Calculus/implicit-function-theorem.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Math-IV-Multivariate-Calculus/implicit-function-theorem.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_implicit_function_theorem_914ceebb_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $h\\colon I\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $C^1$ on an open interval $I$, with $x_0\\in I$. For $G(x,y)=y-h(x)$, the implicit function theorem gives $y=\\varphi(x)$ through $(x_0,h(x_0))$. What is $\\varphi'(x_0)$?","options":["$-h'(x_0)$","$h'(x_0)$","$1$","$\\frac{1}{h'(x_0)}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"35855e961f4f7d6a75e39089","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:01:42.374624+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_implicit_function_theorem_914ceebb_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"On the curve $y^2=x^3+x$, at which listed point does the implicit function theorem fail to give $y$ locally as a function of $x$?","options":["$(1,\\sqrt{2})$","$(2,\\sqrt{10})$","$(0,0)$","None: the curve is smooth everywhere."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f6b7b72e6b25b2f674581ba1","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:01:42.374624+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_implicit_function_theorem_914ceebb_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose the implicit function theorem gives $y=\\varphi(x)$ through a solution $(x_0,y_0)$ of a $C^1$ system $G(x,y)=0$. Applying the chain rule to $G(x,\\varphi(x))=0$, which matrix equation holds at the base point?","options":["$D_xG\\,D\\varphi+D_yG=0$.","$D_xG+D_yG\\,D\\varphi=0$.","$D\\varphi=D_yG\\,D_xG$.","$D_xG+D_yG=0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"033266755c768e62fb8961ec","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:01:42.374624+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_implicit_function_theorem_914ceebb_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The equation $x_1y+y^3+x_2=3$ holds at $(x_1,x_2,y)=(1,1,1)$. With $x_1,x_2$ exogenous, let $y=\\varphi(x_1,x_2)$ be its local implicit solution. What is $\\frac{\\partial\\varphi}{\\partial x_1}(1,1)$?","options":["$-1$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}$","$-\\tfrac{1}{4}$","$-\\tfrac{1}{3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4d25a8bb94bff3a84f6dbf1b","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:01:42.374624+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_implicit_function_theorem_914ceebb_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Near $(x,y)=(1,0)$, the equation $x=\\mathrm{e}^{y}+y$ defines $y=\\varphi(x)$, the inverse of $y\\mapsto \\mathrm{e}^y+y$. Using $G(x,y)=\\mathrm{e}^y+y-x$, what is $\\varphi'(1)$?","options":["$2$","$-\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$1$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ff219cca5af79140c035adab","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:01:42.374624+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_implicit_function_theorem_914ceebb_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The equations $G(x,y)=(y-x)^3=0$ and $H(x,y)=y-x=0$ have the same real solution set. On that set, $G_y=0$ but $H_y=1$. What does this comparison establish?","options":["The derivative condition can fail for a representation whose solution set is a smooth graph.","The derivative condition must fail for every representation of that solution set.","Applying the theorem to $H$ also verifies its hypotheses for $G$.","Equivalent solution sets must have the same endogenous derivative block."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a5f631f2586a8de9f2a1fa89","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:01:42.374624+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_implicit_function_theorem_914ceebb_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $F\\colon\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}^n$ be $C^1$ with $DF(y_0)$ invertible, and put $x_0=F(y_0)$. How does the implicit function theorem, applied to $G(x,y)=F(y)-x=0$ at $(x_0,y_0)$, give a local inverse of $F$?","options":["Solve for $x$: $D_xG=-I$ is always invertible","It cannot: $F$ must be injective on all of $\\mathbb{R}^n$","Solve for $y$, which needs $D_xG$ invertible","Solve for $y$, using that $D_yG=DF(y_0)$ is invertible"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"190dbf9d249282b2b9de7baf","time_limit":45,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:01:42.374624+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_implicit_function_theorem_914ceebb_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Define $G(x,y_1,y_2)=(y_1^2+y_2-x,\\,y_1-y_2)^{\\mathsf T}$. The system $G=0$ holds at $(x,y_1,y_2)=(2,1,1)$, with $x$ exogenous. For its local solution $\\varphi(x)=(y_1(x),y_2(x))^{\\mathsf T}$, what is $D\\varphi(2)$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}-\\tfrac{1}{3}\\\\-\\tfrac{1}{3}\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}\\tfrac{1}{3}\\\\\\tfrac{1}{3}\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}\\tfrac{1}{3}\\\\-\\tfrac{2}{3}\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}\\tfrac{1}{2}\\\\\\tfrac{1}{2}\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7f6f85cbb3b97ca60a6b6f6f","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:01:42.374624+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}